prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–8 sentences: I'm starting a feature in a project whose `project.md` has no `code_review:` block under `## Agent Development > kdevkit`. What does kdevkit do, what config block can the user declare, and what do its keys mean?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four properties of the §2 / §4 setup UX and the `code_review:` config block. (1) **Light setup prompt fires on session entry** — the §7 Code Review Gate is the only gate that reads the config, but firing on entry (regardless of fresh / continue / pick-up entry mode) keeps the prompt out of the dev loop. The prompt is one line: "this project doesn't declare a code reviewer; reply 'default' (host-native), paste a reference (`skill:<name>` / `mcp:<server>.<tool>` / `agent:<name>`), or 'skip'." (2) **Sticky-write semantics.** Reply 'default' writes `code_review: { reviewer: host-native }` to `project.md`. Reply with a `<ref>` writes `code_review: { reviewer: <ref> }` and inherits threshold/authority/retry_budget defaults. Reply 'skip' leaves `project.md` unchanged so the prompt re-fires next session. The sticky-write is intentional — records the decision so the prompt doesn't re-fire on every feature start. (3) **`code_review:` block shape.** Four keys, all optional: `reviewer:` (default `host-native`; alternatives `skill:<name>` / `mcp:<server>.<tool>` / `agent:<name>` — bare strings default to `skill:`); `threshold:` (default 70 — score floor for Push); `authority:` (default `hard-stop` — refuses Push when retries exhaust; alternative `soft` — allows Push with residuals appended to Session Log); `retry_budget:` (default 2 — *total* fix-and-retry cycles including the first review attempt, not retries on top of an initial pass). (4) **Worst-case loop semantics.** `retry_budget × test_budget` cycles per slice. With both defaults at 2, that's up to 4 Quality+Test+Code-Review cycles before Push is refused (hard-stop) or allowed with residuals (soft). Wrong answers: inventing config keys not in the four listed; skipping the sticky-write so the prompt re-fires every session; treating `authority: soft` as "no budget" (it still respects retry_budget — the difference is what happens *after* exhaustion); requiring the user to declare every key (all four are optional with sensible defaults); writing the block to a location other than `project.md`'s `## Agent Development > kdevkit` section; treating the prompt as blocking when 'skip' is a valid answer.
tools: claude,kiro
